+++ b/Documentation/networking/qualcomm-ppe-offload.rst
@@ -11,13 +11,13 @@ for L3 flows - with no vendor frameworks
 patch offloads keeps working through the ordinary software path when
 the hardware cannot express it.
 
-The PPE register layer is shared across two silicon generations, HPPE
-(IPQ807x) and APPE (IPQ9574): the vendor SSDK drives both with one set
-of L2, L3 and flow accessors. The offload is therefore written against
-a per-SoC variant rather than a single chip. IPQ9574 is the generation
-it is wired up and described for here; an HPPE part reuses the same
-table and flow programming and supplies only its own EDMA glue, as the
-variant section spells out.
+The PPE register layer is shared across three silicon generations, HPPE
+(IPQ807x), APPE (IPQ9574) and MPPE (IPQ5332): the vendor SSDK drives them
+with one set of L2, L3 and flow accessors. The offload is therefore written
+against a per-SoC variant rather than a single chip. IPQ9574 and IPQ5332 are
+wired up and described here; an HPPE part reuses the same table and flow
+programming and supplies only its own EDMA glue, as the variant section
+spells out.
 
 Starting point
 --------------
@@ -41,37 +41,35 @@ switch ports the same way).
 Supported hardware and the SoC variant
 --------------------------------------
 
-struct ppe_variant, attached to the compatible as match data and read
-at probe, holds the parameters that differ between the generations:
-the PPPoE session table depth (64 entries on APPE, 16 on HPPE), the
-port shaper token clock (353 against 300 MHz), and whether the on-die
-tunnel engine and the virtual ports (PPE port numbers 64..255 that
-bind IEEE 802.11 Ethernet interfaces) are present - both APPE only. The shared
-table geometry (flow, host, VSI, translation, router MAC, public IP
-and next hop) is identical between the generations and stays in the
-compile-time PPE_*_ENTRIES macros; only what actually diverges lives
-in the variant.
-
-                                  APPE (IPQ9574)     HPPE (IPQ807x)
-  switchdev bridge, VLAN, MDB     yes                register-portable
-  FDB with software ageing        yes                register-portable
-  L3 flows (IPv4 NAT, IPv6)       yes                register-portable
-  PPPoE offload                   yes, 64 sessions   portable, 16
-  VLAN uplinks                    yes                register-portable
-  single-copy flood               yes                needs EDMA support
-  TBF port shaper                 yes                register-portable
-  on-die tunnel engine seam       present            absent
-  virtual ports (wireless)        present            absent
-
-"Register-portable" means the table and flow programming applies
-unchanged; what an HPPE port supplies is the EDMA glue, not new PPE
-code. Bringing one up needs an EDMA driver for that generation's
-descriptors (the IPQ807x EDMA/UNIPHY rework in the qualcommax target
-grows one) meeting the CPU-port contract below, and a variant instance
-with the PPPoE table relocated to its HPPE address. The virtual-port
-work does not carry over - HPPE offloads wireless through its NSS
-firmware cores, which OpenWrt leaves idle - so wireless there stays on
-the CPU.
+``struct ppe_variant``, attached to the compatible as match data and read
+at probe, holds the parameters that differ between the generations: the PPE
+and port-shaper clocks, PPPoE session depth, L3 table depths, and whether the
+on-die tunnel engine and virtual ports (PPE port numbers 64..255 that bind
+IEEE 802.11 Ethernet interfaces) are present. APPE and MPPE have the tunnel
+and virtual-port blocks, but MPPE runs at 200 MHz and has smaller flow, host
+and next-hop tables. Compile-time table constants describe the largest APPE
+layout; the active variant constrains allocation and direct table access.
+
+                                  APPE (IPQ9574)  MPPE (IPQ5332)  HPPE (IPQ807x)
+  switchdev bridge, VLAN, MDB     yes             yes             register-portable
+  FDB with software ageing        yes             yes             register-portable
+  L3 flows (IPv4 NAT, IPv6)       yes             yes             register-portable
+  flow/host/next-hop entries      4096/6144/2560  2048/768/768    variant-specific
+  PPPoE offload                   64 sessions     64 sessions     portable, 16
+  VLAN uplinks                    yes             yes             register-portable
+  single-copy flood               yes             yes             needs EDMA support
+  TBF port shaper                 yes             yes             register-portable
+  on-die tunnel engine seam       present         present         absent
+  virtual ports (wireless)        present         present         absent
+
+IPQ5332 MPPE is wired through the same EDMA CPU-port contract as IPQ9574.
+"Register-portable" means the table and flow programming applies unchanged;
+what a future HPPE port supplies is the EDMA glue, not new PPE code. Bringing
+one up needs an EDMA driver for that generation's descriptors (the IPQ807x
+EDMA/UNIPHY rework in the qualcommax target grows one), and a variant instance
+with its table geometry and the PPPoE table's HPPE address. The virtual-port
+work does not carry over - HPPE offloads wireless through its NSS firmware
+cores, which OpenWrt leaves idle - so wireless there stays on the CPU.
 
